{
  "selected_feature_id": 17,
  "rationale": "Employment status adds strong, broadly state-stable signal for mobility beyond age and education because labor-force attachment is tightly linked to whether and why people move, while being less geography-specific and less shortcut-prone than race, ancestry, income, commute, or citizenship-related variables."
}

{
  "selected_feature_id": 20,
  "rationale": "Personal income adds strong, broadly state-stable signal for mobility beyond age, education, and employment status because residential mobility is tightly linked to economic resources and labor-market opportunity. It is more semantically tied to the decision or ability to move than demographic shortcut variables like race, sex, ancestry, or citizenship, and less geographically brittle than commute time. It also complements ESR by capturing within-employment heterogeneity that remains predictive across states."
}

